The block distinguishes names, values, references, objects and virtual/physical addresses, including object lifetime versus page validity. Nested return-state preservation motivates activation records; process context is distinguished from a procedure call. The new service-level model explicitly differs from the earlier four-bit encoded machine: two trusted process descriptions, sixteen virtual word addresses each, four-word pages and thirty-two physical eight-bit words. Baseline mappings give each process private writable frames and a shared read-only frame; page 3 is absent. Division/remainder derives translation, and disjoint writable-frame sets prove private-memory preservation within the fixed-map model.

The copy service validates every source/destination word before changing memory and snapshots the source for overlap. Exact-integer range checks reject Booleans, out-of-bounds endpoints and negative lengths; empty ranges at valid endpoints require no mapping. Rejected copies return an ordinary error and advance the continuation; forbidden loads/stores stop with an unchanged continuation. Blocking and matching wake events are explicit. Scheduling is an externally supplied finite trace, not a fairness or eventual-completion theorem. The host stores/deep-copies trusted Python objects; no real privilege boundary, concurrent kernel, adversarial host protection or device behavior is claimed.

In the original investigation, process 0 stores 40 at its virtual zero and waits; process 1 stores 90 at its own virtual zero and reads 12 from the shared page. After the matching event, both copy private zero into private page 2 and halt. Eleven instruction steps yield physical words 4,8,20,24 equal to 40,90,40,90, final continuations 6 and 5, and unchanged shared words 12–15. A destination crossing into the read-only page rejects the complete copy without a partial write. An explicit alternative mapping shares writable frame 5: a process-0 store of 77 becomes visible to process 1. This changes the isolation premise rather than contradicting it.

The separate read-only two-line, one-word cache has declared lookup cost 1 and additional miss cost 5: total N+5M, compared with direct cost 5N. Four hits/four misses cost 28 versus 40; eight alternating conflicts cost 48. A history-based reference checks the tag-array implementation. These are stipulated model costs, not real elapsed times or measured hardware specifications.

